# Line endings: LF in the repository and LF in every working copy.
#
# Prettier writes LF, and `format:check` compares against LF. With Git for
# Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true`, every file came out of a checkout with
# CRLF — so on Windows the check reported every file in the tree, and the only
# changes that mattered were lost among them. Stated here, the rule no longer
# depends on how each machine's Git happens to be configured.
#
# Nothing in the repository changes: what is committed was already LF, because
# autocrlf converts on the way in. An existing working copy keeps its CRLF files
# until they are checked out again (`git add --renormalize .` followed by a
# fresh checkout of anything unmodified).
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
